# Frontend environment.
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. This file documents NAMES ONLY — never commit
# a value here (CLAUDE.md section 6).
#
# Nothing in this application may be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ unless it is
# genuinely safe in the browser: that prefix inlines the value into the client
# bundle at build time. Nothing auth-related belongs in the browser at all
# (invariant 9).

# Origin of the Django API, used server-side by the /api/* rewrite in
# next.config.ts. Deliberately NOT public — the internal API hostname should
# not ship to the browser.
#   http://localhost:8000   running the backend directly
#   http://api:8000         inside the compose network
# Defaults to http://localhost:8000 when unset.
API_ORIGIN=

# How a playback id becomes a URL, for the lesson player. Public on purpose:
# it is a URL template, not a credential, and the token that authorises
# playback is minted per request by the API.
#
# Two placeholders are substituted: {playback_id} and {token}. Unset — which is
# the case today, because no video provider has been chosen (M5 ships a fake
# one) — makes the lesson page fall back to a stand-in clock that reports
# progress exactly as a real player would.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAYBACK_URL_TEMPLATE=

# The site's own public origin, for canonical URLs, sitemap.xml and robots.txt.
#
# Server-only: every consumer runs during the build or on the server, and the
# browser never needs it. That the origin ends up visible IN the HTML is not a
# reason to ship it TO the bundle.
#
# **Unset is the current, expected state, and it is deliberately safe.** No
# domain has been chosen — ADR-025 settled the hosting, not the name. Unset
# means: no canonical tags, an empty sitemap, and `Disallow: /` in robots.txt.
# Nothing gets indexed, which is recoverable; the alternative of defaulting to
# localhost would publish canonicals pointing at a machine no crawler can
# reach, which is not, and is silent.
#
# A value that is set but malformed — no scheme, or a scheme other than
# http/https — stops the build rather than being ignored. A typo is somebody
# configuring this and getting it wrong, which is a different situation from
# nobody having configured it yet. See src/lib/site.ts and ADR-035.
#
# A localhost or .test origin is treated as valid for building URLs and never
# indexable, so a production build previewed locally cannot invite crawlers.
#   https://lingua.example    once a domain exists
SITE_URL=

# Error reporting — Sentry (M14 T5, ADR-027).
#
# Public on purpose, and it meets the bar this file sets above: a browser DSN
# is a write-only ingest key. It authorises sending an event and can read
# nothing back, which is why Sentry publishes it in a client bundle by design.
#
# **Baked in at build time**, like API_ORIGIN, so one build cannot serve two
# environments — the argument CLAUDE.md §11 #4 turns on.
#
# Give this its own Sentry project, separate from the backend's. The Developer
# plan allows 5k errors a month across everything and offers no spend
# threshold, so separate projects are the only way to mute one noisy tier
# without muting all of them.
#
# Empty means the browser SDK never initialises. Unset is the normal state here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=

# The server-side DSN, read by instrumentation.ts.
#
# **Read the note in instrumentation.ts before relying on this.** It works
# under `next dev` and `next start`; it does not reach the Cloudflare Worker,
# which is measured rather than assumed. Setting it does no harm and buys
# nothing on the current deployment target.
SENTRY_DSN=
